var rf = ee.Classifier.smileRandomForest({
  numberOfTrees: 100,
  seed: 7
}).train({
  features: training,
  classProperty: 'class',
  inputProperties: predictors.bandNames()
});

var classified = predictors.classify(rf);
var validated = validation.classify(rf);
var testCM = validated.errorMatrix('class', 'classification');






Figure 7.4: Random forest land-cover classification of London from Sentinel-2. The strongest pattern is the dense urban core along the Thames, while the suburban fringe looks more mixed and patchy because many pixels combine vegetation and built surfaces.









Figure 7.5: Accuracy assessment from the Google Earth Engine console. The reported validation accuracy is high, but should be interpreted cautiously because the train-test split was done at pixel level within the sampled polygons.









